"01_view_overrides.mst — View overrides that must load AFTER st/mvp
 (DolphinDart DD10).

 LAYER ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, for the third time in this project (the DD9
 alias globals and the depth-prefixed emission were the first two). These
 methods override ones the TRANSLATED `UI.View` defines, so a file in
 `st/dolphin_compat` — which loads before `st/mvp` — is simply overwritten by
 the class it is trying to adjust. This layer loads last:

   st/world;st/dolphin_compat;st/prims/rt;st/prims/structs;st/prims;
   st/prims/aliases;st/mvp;st/mvp_compat

 Nothing here reimplements Dolphin logic. Each method replaces a call into a
 part of Dolphin this port does not have, with the substrate's equivalent."

"── the class the door owns ─────────────────────────────────────────────"
nil subclass: View [
    "Dolphin registers a window class per View subclass and answers its name.
     Here every View is created against the DOOR's class, because its WndProc
     is the one that reflects messages into the image. Registering a second
     class with our WndProc would work identically and buy nothing."
    View class >> winClassName [ ^Win32 mvpTopClassName ]
    View class >> wndClassName [ ^Win32 mvpTopClassName ]

    "Dolphin asks the desktop for a creation parent when none is set, and then
     TALKS to it: `CreateWindowFunction>>scaleToDpiOf:` sends
     `scaleChildCreation:` to the parent. So the desktop cannot be nil — it
     has to be an object that answers the parent protocol."
    View class >> desktop [ ^DesktopView current ]
]

"── the desktop ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 The creation parent of a top-level window. Its HWND is 0 (CreateWindowExW
 takes 0 for `no parent`), and its DPI is 96 — the DD9 brief's recorded trim,
 `dpi ^96`, no per-monitor awareness in v1. `scaleChildCreation:` is therefore
 the identity: there is nothing to scale between 96 and 96, and inventing a
 factor here would move every window by a number nobody chose."
Object subclass: DesktopView [
    <classVars: Current>
    DesktopView class >> current [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    asParameter [ ^0 ]
    handle [ ^0 ]
    dpi [ ^96 ]
    scaleChildCreation: aCreateWindow [ ^aCreateWindow ]
    isOpen [ ^true ]
    parentView [ ^nil ]
    "`ShellView>>initialize` sets its parent to the desktop and then asks the
     parent whether it IS the desktop, to decide whether it is a top-level
     window. Answering true is the whole point of this object."
    isDesktop [ ^true ]
    view [ ^self ]
]


"── running Dolphin's class-side initialize ─────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's package loader sends `initialize` to a class after filing it in,
 and six classes in this wave rely on it: `View class >> initialize` sets
 `NextId := 0`, builds the MessageMap and makes the ViewClosedError — without
 it `View>>defaultId` does `nil + 1`.

 The layer loader has no package concept, so this is the explicit equivalent.
 It is NOT automatic-on-load by design: the order matters (a class-side
 initialize may read another class's variables), and a list that is read and
 checked beats a traversal whose order nobody controls."
Object subclass: DolphinBoot [
    "A brace array: `Array with:` stops at four arguments in this dialect."
    DolphinBoot class >> classesToInitialize [
        ^{ Rectangle. Color. CommandDescription. View. ShellView. Menu }
    ]

    "Answers the classes that FAILED, so a caller can assert on the list rather
     than on a count. Each is attempted independently: a class whose
     `initialize` reaches something this port has not translated yet must not
     stop the ones that would have worked. Today that is `Color` (needs the
     colour-table classes) and `CommandDescription` (wants a weak lookup
     table) — both recorded here rather than quietly dropped from the list,
     because the list is the statement of what SHOULD initialize."
    DolphinBoot class >> initializeClasses [
        | failed |
        failed := OrderedCollection new.
        self classesToInitialize do: [ :each |
            (each respondsTo: #initialize)
                ifTrue: [
                    [ each initialize ]
                        on: Error
                        do: [ :e | failed add: each name ] ] ].
        ^failed asArray
    ]

    "The ones that must work for a View to be created at all."
    DolphinBoot class >> initializeViewClasses [
        Rectangle initialize. View initialize. ShellView initialize.
        ^true
    ]
]
